# {{IdpName}} federated sign-in

This wrapper authenticates users via OAuth 2.0 + PKCE against **{{IdpName}}**.
The generated `OAuthAuthenticator` opens the system browser, listens on a
short-lived loopback port, and persists the resulting tokens via the local
session store (DPAPI on Windows, `dotnet user-secrets` elsewhere).

## 1. Register an OAuth application with {{IdpName}}

Create an OAuth client of type **Desktop** / **Native** / **Installed app**
(the exact label varies by IdP - "Desktop app" for Google, "Mobile and
desktop applications" for Microsoft Entra ID, "Other" for Facebook).
PKCE-only public clients do not need a client secret.

When prompted for **Authorized redirect URIs**, register the loopback form:

```
http://localhost/callback
```

The wrapper chooses a free port at runtime; most IdPs (including {{IdpName}})
accept any port on `http://localhost` once the path is registered, per
RFC 8252 section 7.3.

## 2. Required scopes

At minimum request `openid`, `email`, and `profile`. Add any IdP-specific
scopes your wrapper needs (e.g. `https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar`
for Google Calendar, `User.Read` for Microsoft Graph, `email` for Facebook).
Configure them via the `{{IdpName}}Scopes` token at generation time.

## 3. Configure the client id

The generator bakes the client id into the emitted authenticator. If you
need to rotate it, regenerate the wrapper or override the constant before
publishing.

If your scenario requires a confidential client (rare for desktop apps),
store the client secret with:

```pwsh
dotnet user-secrets set "{{IdpName}}:ClientSecret" "<secret>"
```

on macOS/Linux, or rely on DPAPI on Windows.

## 4. First-run experience

On first invocation the wrapper will:

1. Print the authorize URL to stderr.
2. Launch the system browser.
3. Wait for the {{IdpName}} consent screen to redirect back to
   `http://localhost:<port>/callback`.
4. Exchange the code for tokens and store them via the cross-platform
   session store.

Subsequent runs read tokens from the store; expired access tokens are
silently refreshed using the stored refresh token.

## 5. Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Likely cause |
|---|---|
| `redirect_uri_mismatch` | The loopback URI is not registered with {{IdpName}}. Re-check step 1; the path must be exactly `/callback`. |
| `invalid_grant` on refresh | The refresh token was revoked or expired. Delete the stored session (`dotnet user-secrets clear` or remove the DPAPI blob) and re-run to trigger an interactive login. |
| Browser opens but never returns | A firewall is blocking loopback. Confirm `http://localhost:<port>/` is reachable from the same machine. |
| `invalid_client` | The client id baked into the wrapper does not match the one in the {{IdpName}} console. |